The fork that matters: this gives ranked keyword matching but no typo tolerance (e.g. "wirless" won't match "wireless"). If that matters for the product catalog, add the `pg_trgm` extension and a trigram index as a fallback/supplement — that's the next decision, not needed to ship v1.

Key on `client_id`, not IP — `routes/api.js` already resolves `req.client.client_id` from the API key before your handlers run, and the auth comment explicitly says clients share IPs behind NAT and one client spans many IPs, so IP-based limiting would both overblock innocent tenants and underblock the offender.

State has to live in Redis, not in-process — `app.js` forks 4 workers per box with nothing shared in module scope, so an in-memory counter would let a hammering client get 4x (or more, across boxes) the intended limit by round-robining through workers. You already have a shared client in `redis.js`; reuse it rather than standing up a second store.

Do the check atomically in Redis (INCR+PEXPIRE race under concurrent requests from the same client, so use a Lua script) — a sliding-window or token-bucket script keyed on `rl:{client_id}` that increments and checks the limit in one round trip:

```js
// pseudocode, not wired in
const allowed = await redis.client.eval(TOKEN_BUCKET_LUA, 1, `rl:${client.client_id}`, capacity, refillRate, now);
if (!allowed) return res.status(429).set("Retry-After", "1").end();
```

Wire it as the first thing in `router.use`, right after `req.client` is set and before any `db.query` — that's what actually protects other clients, since the DB round-trips are the shared resource being degraded.

Trade-off: fixed-window INCR/EXPIRE is simpler but lets a client burst 2x at window boundaries; token-bucket via Lua avoids that at the cost of one small script to maintain. For a client actively hammering you, I'd take the token bucket.

